  "cards": [
    {"dot": "cyan", "title": "What continue means in each mode", "items": ["An allow continues. In enforce mode a policy deny returns its status without forwarding. In monitor mode native denies become would-deny and continue; configured CRS matches also continue (firewall.go:662-692; inspection.go:31-41).", "Monitor mode still rejects malformed input, structural validation failures, resource limits and evaluation errors (docs/APPLICATION_FIREWALL.md:72-77).", "CRS is an optional build/config branch. The request must pass both the native policy and enabled inspection before reaching the origin (firewall.go:502-525)."]},
    {"dot": "amber", "title": "The capture bridge cannot intercept a past transaction", "items": ["The lower lane is a separate retrospective entry point. -firewall-policy evaluates decoded records after observation and does not intercept completed transactions (cmd/capture/flags.go:47).", "Rootless HTTP mode installs no host packet rules. Optional NFQUEUE verdicts and nftables leases belong to separate Linux commands in an explicitly named namespace (docs/APPLICATION_FIREWALL.md:85-103).", "Origin access must be restricted to the proxy; traffic sent directly to the origin bypasses this WAF path (docs/APPLICATION_FIREWALL.md:5)."]},
    {"dot": "emerald", "title": "Request path scope", "items": ["This chart focuses on request admission. Response headers are inspected separately; response bodies need inspectResponse=true. WebSocket, gRPC and HTTP/3 have explicit listener/inspection contracts (docs/APPLICATION_FIREWALL.md:122-129).", "File, managed and fleet policy ownership are distinct operating modes; managed and fleet ownership cannot overlap (docs/APPLICATION_FIREWALL.md:85-102).", "Next: rule-evaluation.html explains audit-record rules; those are distinct from netcap-policy/v1 WAF policy."]}
  ]
